# Closed-app notifications and reminders

{{ project }} requires our own Hermes Agent fork at [{{ fork_repository }}]({{ fork_url }}). It receives Android notifications for completed or failed runs, pending approvals, questions, and reminders. Each registered device gets a generic alert. Opening it requires the appropriate profile before the app can read the associated thread or job. The bridge sends the event kind and opaque event and installation identifiers. Web Push also encrypts the expiry time so the phone can discard a cached notice after the retention window. A profile can offer Firebase Cloud Messaging, a self-hosted ntfy distributor, or both. [Set up UnifiedPush](unifiedpush.md)

## Connect a profile

**One single HTTPS server hostname** (such as `https://hermes.example.com`) serves all users and profiles under `/p/<profile>`—no separate HTTPS hostname per user is needed. In the single-host container deployment (`deploy/accounts.py`), each profile has its own isolated Hermes home, access key, and push bridge state; Caddy strips `/p/<profile>`, forwards `/api/mobile/push` and its children to that profile’s bridge, and forwards the remaining Hermes API paths to that profile’s gateway. In the Android app, enter the shared server HTTPS address and your **Server profile** name so the app composes `https://hermes.example.com/p/<profile>` automatically. Each bridge process observes one isolated profile home and authenticates with that profile’s `API_SERVER_KEY`.

For self-hosted delivery, configure the ntfy origin and VAPID contact. The bridge publishes to that origin and the ntfy Android app listens there, including on AOSP devices without Google Play services. A UnifiedPush-only bridge needs no Google credentials or Google network access. [Self-hosted setup and private-server permissions](unifiedpush.md)

For Firebase delivery, register the APK’s Android application ID in your Firebase project, enable the Cloud Messaging API and give the bridge service account permission to send messages. Supply the four values from the registered Android application's Firebase configuration. Application Default Credentials on the server authorize FCM HTTP v1 through Google's OAuth client. When FCM is enabled, the bridge loads these credentials at startup and needs outbound access to its Google authentication and messaging services. The Android SDK configuration can be returned to the authenticated phone. [Firebase authorization](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/auth-server)

| Server environment | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `HERMES_HOME` | This user's runtime directory, readable by the bridge. Mount read-only. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_STATE` | Persistent writable directory, mode 0700. The image uses `/var/lib/hermes-mobile-push`. |
| `API_SERVER_KEY` | Same strong bearer key as this user's Hermes API. |
| `MOBILE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID` | FCM only. Firebase project ID. |
| `MOBILE_FIREBASE_SENDER_ID` | FCM only. Firebase project number. |
| `MOBILE_FIREBASE_APP_ID` | FCM only. Android Firebase application identifier, typically starting with `1:`. |
| `MOBILE_FIREBASE_API_KEY` | FCM only. Android application’s Firebase API key. |
| `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS` | FCM only. Path to a private, read-only credential file when using file-backed ADC. Workload credentials are also supported. |
| `MOBILE_UNIFIEDPUSH_ORIGIN` | UnifiedPush only. HTTPS origin of the ntfy server. |
| `MOBILE_VAPID_SUBJECT` | UnifiedPush only. Operator contact for the VAPID signature, such as `mailto:operator@example.org`. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_BIND`, `MOBILE_PUSH_PORT` | Listen address and port. Default `127.0.0.1` and `8766`. A container can bind its private network interface behind the authenticated HTTPS origin. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_POLL_SECONDS` | Ledger observation interval, default 2 seconds. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_RECONCILE_SECONDS` | Interval between retained-history sweeps, default 600 seconds. Normal observations read recent changes with a two-poll overlap. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_RETENTION_SECONDS` | Time to retain events and attempt delivery, default 604800 seconds. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS` | Maximum retry backoff, default 3600 seconds. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_SEND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` | HTTP send timeout for FCM and UnifiedPush, default 10 seconds. |
| `MOBILE_PUSH_LOG_LEVEL` | Python log level, default `INFO`. |

Build from `server/Dockerfile` using `server/` as the context. The image runs as UID and GID 10000. The bridge needs read access to the isolated gateway’s SQLite files. For a local Python installation use Python 3.11 or newer, install `server/requirements.txt` into a virtual environment, then run `python -m mobile_push` from `server/`.

For FCM, use a device with Google Play services. The Android SDK binds its default Firebase Messaging instance to one project per app installation. Multiple Hermes accounts on one phone can share that Firebase project while keeping distinct installation UUIDs, management keys and profile bearers. Disable the previous project’s enrollments before switching projects. FCM can deliver data messages to the native receiver after the activity is dismissed or Android evicts the process. FCM high priority is reserved for user-visible notifications. [FCM priorities](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/android/message-priority)

For either transport, configure notifications from the app and grant the Android notification permission. Android Settings force-stop requires manually reopening the app. Offline delivery depends on network access, the event retention window and provider delivery. [Android notification permission](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/notifications/notification-permission) · [Force-stop behavior](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/flutter/receive-messages)

## Hermes event sources

The bridge reads the fork's profile-local databases (`runs_idempotency.db`, `cron/executions.db`, and `mobile_state.db`). It can re-read recent entries after restart and places events and per-device deliveries into a single SQLite transaction. Android run starts use a stable `Idempotency-Key` with `POST /v1/runs`. Hermes writes these reservations and their authenticated status to `runs_idempotency.db`. The bridge selects only that API bearer’s run scope and reads run, session, status and prompt identity. Each distinct pending approval or question can generate an alert. The canonical gateway persists opaque pending prompt identities with each owned run. The bridge retracts queued alerts when their prompt settles, is replaced, or its run stops. A request already in flight to a provider can still arrive after settlement. Opening it retrieves the current state. Approvals that were already answered between ledger observations need no response. Completed and failed runs are retained by their stable run ID. Room-grant runs use a separate Hermes namespace.

For proactive agent deliveries (`mobile_deliver` in our fork), the bridge polls unacknowledged rows in `<HERMES_HOME>/mobile_state.db` (`delivery_id`, `kind`, `urgency`, `session_id`, `created_at`) and emits only opaque metadata plus a hashed `profile_hint_opaque`. Delivery titles and JSON payloads never leave the gateway volume; Android fetches and renders them in plain language inside **Notes & tasks → Inbox** only after the owning profile unlocks. Supported urgency levels (`normal`, `high`, `silent`) integrate with per-category preferences and **Quiet Hours**, where silent or deferred notices wait quietly in the inbox without buzzing the phone.

On Android, a pending question appears as **Hermes has a question**. Unlocking the matching profile opens the conversation and fetches its live choices from Hermes. When a question or approval settles or leaves the history window, the bridge sends a silent withdrawal to each installation that may have received the prompt. Each withdrawal gets a fresh transport window of `MOBILE_PUSH_RETENTION_SECONDS`. The bridge can retry it across restarts after erasing the original history entry. The withdrawal contains the same opaque installation and event identifiers. Android remembers it even when it arrives before the original alert. A displayed question or approval also expires locally after 24 hours if the phone misses the withdrawal. The question, answer and choices stay on the authenticated gateway route.

Create and manage reminders with the normal Hermes `/api/jobs` API. Set delivery to `local` to keep the job output in Hermes. When a scheduled or manually triggered job actually starts, Hermes records an execution in `cron/executions.db`. That execution creates one reminder event and can be opened by its job ID. The scheduler must be active for a reminder to fire. The bridge observes every job in that user's isolated runtime. Offline reconciliation is bounded by both the bridge retention period and the fork’s own ledger retention. [Hermes run writer](https://github.com/LLM-4-People/hermes-agent/blob/c7a3fe245d6bf610f73ff555d57e4ed632f8ded8/gateway/platforms/api_server_runs.py#L312) · [Cron execution ledger](https://github.com/LLM-4-People/hermes-agent/blob/c7a3fe245d6bf610f73ff555d57e4ed632f8ded8/cron/executions.py#L329)

## Enrollment and synchronization

All responses are private and carry `Cache-Control: no-store`. Discovery and event reads require the profile bearer. Each local profile generates its own random UUIDv4 installation ID and random 32-byte management key. Send the management key as 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters. Keep it on the device in protected storage for registration updates while the profile is locked.

| Request | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /api/mobile/push` | Lists enabled `providers` (`fcm`, `unifiedpush`, or both). When FCM is enabled, discovery also returns `provider: "fcm"` for earlier clients. The `firebase` object contains `applicationId`, `gcmSenderId`, `projectId`, and `apiKey`. For `unifiedpush`, the public signing key is in `unifiedpush.vapidPublicKey`. |
| `PUT /api/mobile/push/devices/{id}` | For FCM, enroll with the profile bearer and a JSON body containing `token` and `management_key`. The token is the registration token supplied by the app's Firebase Messaging version. A 204 confirms persistence. |
| `PUT /api/mobile/push/devices/{id}` with device bearer | For FCM, rotate the destination with the management key as bearer and a JSON body containing only `token`. A 409 means the token belongs to another installation in this runtime. |
| `DELETE /api/mobile/push/devices/{id}` | Revoke the device and its queued deliveries. The profile bearer receives 204 even for an absent device. A well-formed device bearer receives 204 after removing its own matching binding, including on retry when the binding is already gone. Other devices’ enrollments stay private. |
| `GET /api/mobile/push/events/{event_id}` | Returns event kind, timestamp and nullable `run_id`, `session_id`, `job_id`. Using an unknown or expired ID returns 404. |
| `GET /api/mobile/push/events?after=0&limit=50` | Returns `events`, `next_cursor` and `has_more` in insertion order. Retain the cursor per profile and fetch while `has_more` is true. Maximum page size is 100. |

For UnifiedPush, the same device routes reserve an installation and bind or rotate its Web Push subscription. [UnifiedPush device contract](unifiedpush.md#device-contract)

A provider acceptance settles an outbox row. A network error leaves it queued with backoff. The sender honors the provider’s `Retry-After` header within the event retention window. Deferred delivery logs identify the transport, local reason or HTTP status, attempt count and scheduled delay. If a worker exits after the provider accepts a request, a retry can arrive with the same event ID. Device handling must deduplicate using both the installation and event IDs. An unregistered-token response removes that exact device registration, with a lease check to protect a newer registration. Opening an alert only navigates. Approval decisions go through the existing authenticated run approval API.

## Verify delivery

Run `python -m unittest discover -s server/tests` from the repository root after installing the server dependencies. To also exercise a locally installed Hermes fork, set `HERMES_TEST_CHECKOUT` to its checkout in an environment with the fork’s Python dependencies. Use the matching fork’s test environment for the optional contracts. They create run and cron rows through Hermes’s real writers, resolve a canonical test approval and question, and inspect the bridge’s outgoing FCM envelopes through a local transport capture.

On an enrolled Android device, launch a run, dismiss the activity and inspect the notification drawer after completion. Open the alert, unlock its profile and confirm its thread. Repeat for a scheduled reminder, then check that a second isolated user receives neither event. Deny notification permission and confirm that the authenticated event feed still recovers the event. Revoke the device and verify its old management key receives 401 for token rotation and 204 for a replayed removal. Later runs should produce no alert there. Run these device checks with an enrolled installation, a real gateway and the selected push transport. The FCM route requires a provisioned Firebase project.
